 ],
 "literal": "Like fashioned ornaments, the golden-rimmed wheel's / bright edge cutting, thick petals shortened / short-stemmed water lilies also has here / in the seashore grove where the chariot-rider went.",
 "translation": "In this seashore grove where the chariot-rider passed, even the short-stemmed water lilies have their thick petals cut short, severed by the bright edge of his golden-rimmed wheels that look like crafted ornaments.",
 "modern": "ஆபரணம் போலச் செய்யப்பட்ட பொன்னாலான விளிம்பைக் கொண்ட தேர்ச் சக்கரம், தனது ஒளிரும் விளிம்பால் வெட்டியதால், தடித்த இதழ்கள் குறைந்துபோன குட்டையான நெய்தல் மலர்களையும் உடையதாக இருக்கிறது, தேரினை உடைய தலைவன் சென்றுபோன இந்த நெய்தல் நிலத்துக் கடற்கரைச் சோலை.",
 "explanation": "The friend of the heroine speaks this poem within the hearing of the hero, who has been visiting secretly by chariot. She describes how the bright, sharp edges of his golden-rimmed chariot wheels have sliced the thick petals of the short-stemmed water lilies (neytal) in the coastal grove. This vivid image of the landscape serves as an implicit metaphor (uḷḷurai). Just as the chariot wheels repeatedly run over and damage the delicate flowers on the path, the hero's frequent, secretive visits and the resulting gossip are harming the heroine's health and reputation. By pointing out the damaged flowers, the friend subtly urges the hero to stop these transient visits and marry the heroine formally.",
